Z||000001||ray b||02-10-2001||01:30 AM||||stock no autos no after market for racer priced at 1200-1500 for stick only. but do you need it as no tork reaction as motor is not twisted to one side just front to rear.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phaeton||02-10-2001||12:36 PM||||Dry pavement under the left tire, dust and rubble under the right.  I've been looking for a locker also with no luck.<BR>The price I'm willing to pay has been creeping up also.<BR>Does anybody work where they have access to used airlockers?  If brake components are being machined to fit then there is no reason some work can't be done to make a locking unit fit a Fiero differential.<BR>1800 members, the experience needed to do this exists within our membership.<P>The motivation lies in the snowy climates where knowing the rear will pop out everytime the wheels are spun is actually a safety issue.  Guessing the rear will slide and having one wheel spin madly while the other holds the corner (or vice versa) can put the Fiero in the snowbank.<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000003||Wannabe||02-10-2001||01:14 PM||||There is an LSD available for the Isuzu tranny. I while back, I posted a note about someone looking to put together a group of buyers for an LSD from Quaife for the Getrag. Apparently, Quaife will build them if they get orders for six or eight or something like that, adn I think the price was in the $1600 range. Maybe do a search in the archives for the post. Also, I think the info originated from a place called Bill's Isuzu Performance on the web. I find it hard to believe there wouldn't be more interest in thins, especially considering the growing popularity of 3800 and 3800 SC conversions for Cavaliers. <p>[This message has been edited by Wannabe (edited 02-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||DrRodey||02-10-2001||09:19 PM||||Hold up guys.. don't get too many wild ideas.  By puttin on a locker, locking both axles together, and having both wheels spin at the same time all the time, You will lose a little more tracion on corners.  Locking diffs are great for drags where you don't have to turn, but the diff in the pumpkin (well baby pumpkin in our cases) was made to slip on pourpose.  Even at high speeds, when going around a corner one wheel will rotate a little slower just due to the extra forced exerted on it, pulling the back end of the car around with you.  When you lock it up, your back end will become Extremely loose.  ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Hank is Here||02-11-2001||01:29 AM||||Rodey,<BR>there is a big difference between a LSD and a Locker. I think what rnoswal is thinking about is a LSD not a locking diff.  ||||reg||1
